 \subsection{Effect of the Multi-Head Codebook (MHC)}
 Finally, we investigated the structural advantage of the continuous latent logic by disabling the Multi-Head Codebook (the ``No MHC'' run). Operating purely as a standard continuous bottleneck without the specialized multi-head attention routing led to a marginal but noticeable degradation in optimization efficiency. Both the Mel Loss (1.557 vs 1.541) and the JEPA Loss (0.0089 vs 0.0088) increased. This validates that the continuous latent parameterization efficiently routes and stabilizes phonemic structures prior to decoding. The MHC acts as a soft-clustering mechanism that naturally groups similar acoustic phenomena into distinct heads, allowing the model to represent complex phonetic boundaries with significantly greater precision than a monolithic continuous bottleneck.
 
